1.) mvn clean package dockerfile:build
    cd docker && docker-compose -p chapter5 up

2.) 1.) $defaultConfig = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri "http://localhost:8071/licensing-service/default" -Method Get

        $defaultConfig | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10

    2.) $devConfig = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri "http://localhost:8071/licensing-service/dev" -Method Get

        $devConfig | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10

    3.) "DEFAULT CONFIGURATION"

        $defaultConfig.propertySources | ForEach-Object {
            [PSCustomObject]@{
                Source = $_.name
                ExampleProperty = $_.source.'example.property'
            }
        }

        "DEV CONFIGURATION"

        $devConfig.propertySources | ForEach-Object {
            [PSCustomObject]@{
                Source = $_.name
                ExampleProperty = $_.source.'example.property'
            }
        }

4.) What to learn from it
    The licensing service's 
    own application code contains zero 
    database credentials — its bootstrap.yml 
    holds only its name, profile, and the Config Server URI. 
    Everything environment-specific (datasource URL, credentials, feature properties) 
    lives centrally, per profile, encrypted where sensitive. 
    In principle the same Docker image can run in dev or prod 
    just by changing the active profile. Caveat for this code:
    the compose file sets PROFILE and CONFIGSERVER_URI, 
    but the Dockerfile's ENTRYPOINT never reads them. 
    The profile comes from bootstrap.yml (hard-coded dev). 
    Setting SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: prod in the compose file 
    would switch it, but there is no ostock_prod database, 
    so the service would fail to start.